        <h1 class="page-title">Small Claims Court Guide</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">How to resolve disputes without hiring a lawyer</p>

        <p>Small claims court is designed to let everyday people resolve disputes involving relatively modest amounts of money quickly, affordably, and without needing an attorney. If you're owed money by a landlord, contractor, business, or another individual, small claims court might be your best option.</p>

        <h2>What Is Small Claims Court?</h2>
        <p>Small claims court is a simplified version of civil court designed for disputes involving smaller dollar amounts. The process is intentionally less formal than regular court, with simplified rules of evidence and procedure so that people without legal training can represent themselves effectively.</p>

        <h2>Filing Limits by State</h2>
        <p>Every state sets its own maximum dollar amount you can sue for in small claims court. These limits typically range from $2,500 to $25,000 depending on the state. If your claim exceeds the limit, you may need to either reduce your claim to fit within the limit or file in regular civil court instead.</p>

        <h2>Common Reasons to File</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Unreturned security deposits</li>
          <li>Unpaid wages or overdue invoices</li>
          <li>Property damage caused by another person</li>
          <li>Breach of contract disputes</li>
          <li>Disputes with contractors over incomplete or poor quality work</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How the Process Works</h2>
        <p>Generally, you'll file a claim form at your local courthouse and pay a filing fee, which is usually quite low, often under $100. The court will then notify the other party ("the defendant") of the claim and schedule a hearing date. Both sides present their case to a judge, who typically rules on the spot or within a few days.</p>

        <h2>Tips for Winning Your Case</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Bring all relevant documents — contracts, receipts, photos, text messages</li>
          <li>Organize your evidence in a clear, chronological order</li>
          <li>Bring witnesses if they can support your version of events</li>
          <li>Be concise and stick to the facts when speaking to the judge</li>
          <li>Send a formal demand letter before filing, as some courts require proof you tried to resolve it first</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What Happens After You Win</h2>
        <p>Winning your case doesn't automatically guarantee payment. If the other party doesn't pay voluntarily, you may need to take additional steps to collect, such as wage garnishment or placing a lien on their property. Each state has its own collection procedures, so it's worth researching this before filing.</p>

